<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">NKVD</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1934">1934</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as People's Commissariat for Internal Affairs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The NKVD (People's Commissariat for Internal Affairs) was the Soviet interior ministry which, from 1934, absorbed the political police and ran the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> labour camps. Under Genrikh Yagoda, Nikolai Yezhov and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavrentiy-beria">Lavrentiy Beria</a> it carried out the Great Purge of 1936–1938, in which about 680,000 people were shot, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportations</a> of whole nationalities. It was renamed the MVD in 1946.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nkvd.jpg" alt="NKVD" width="320" height="529" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: jgaray · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblema_NKVD.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
